    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Neon Lights is a 2001 covers album by Simple Minds, released on Eagle Records, and it finds the band reinterpreting songs that had shaped their musical identity. Rather than presenting original material, the record works as a curated homage, with versions of tracks by Van Morrison, David Bowie, Pete Shelley, Patti Smith, Kraftwerk, The Doors, Echo & the Bunnymen, Neil Young, Roxy Music, and the Velvet Underground.\n\nMusically, the album sits at the intersection of electronic and rock, with a synth-pop lean that suits Simple Minds’ polished late-period style. The band gives familiar songs a spacious, atmospheric treatment, and the inclusion of covers like Kraftwerk’s “Neon Lights” and Joy Division’s “Love Will Tear Us Apart” in some editions underlines the album’s emphasis on modern rock and electronic touchstones. It was also issued as a warm-up for the next studio album, Cry, making it a reflective, transitional release in the group’s catalog.",
